- Thank you.

00:02:01.338 --> 00:02:04.284
India, let's get into

00:02:04.284 --> 00:02:06.281
the show!

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:09.912
- Show.

00:02:09.912 --> 00:02:11.395
- I hope your sparkling water's delicious.

00:02:11.395 --> 00:02:12.695
- [India] It's really good, thank you.

00:02:12.695 --> 00:02:14.077
Good, let's do it!

00:02:14.077 --> 00:02:15.011
- [India] From Joe--
- [Gary] Joe.

00:02:15.011 --> 00:02:17.450
Oh, by the way, before
we get into the show.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.985
We released this morning a
very important film to me.

00:02:20.985 --> 00:02:23.016
Way too many people think that

00:02:23.016 --> 00:02:26.023
entrepreneurship is a young man's sport

00:02:26.023 --> 00:02:28.766
and it is the absolute golden era,

00:02:28.766 --> 00:02:30.574
much like an golden era of this show,

00:02:30.574 --> 00:02:33.723
the golden era for 40 to
80-year-old entrepreneurs.

00:02:33.723 --> 00:02:36.934
So Staphon, put it up right
here, let it play a little bit.

00:02:36.934 --> 00:02:38.700
Actually, as a matter of
fact, can you do this?

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.493
Yeah, of course you can do this.

00:02:39.493 --> 00:02:41.789
Just let it play a little bit
while we do this first question.

00:02:41.789 --> 00:02:42.968
Just have it right here,

00:02:42.968 --> 00:02:44.776
play a little bit while we do the show.

00:02:44.776 --> 00:02:47.376
Let's link it up in YouTube and Facebook

00:02:47.376 --> 00:02:49.408
and, more importantly, so many of you

00:02:49.408 --> 00:02:51.501
have 40 to 80-year-old entrepreneurs

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:53.167
in your friends and family network.

00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:54.772
You may not realize they're
entrepreneurs because they

00:02:54.772 --> 00:02:56.842
grew up in a time where
that wasn't practical.

00:02:56.842 --> 00:03:00.256
I'd like you to watch "6
Minutes for the Next 60 Years."

00:03:00.256 --> 00:03:01.739
I like how we went with it.

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:03.100
All right, India.
- [India] Um,from Joe.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:04.847
- Joe.
- [India] "In a tactic-obsessed world,

00:03:04.847 --> 00:03:07.143
"how do you hammer home the
truth that WineLibraryTV

00:03:07.143 --> 00:03:10.434
"succeeded because of you,
not the daily videos?"

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:11.349
- Interesting.

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:15.970
So Joe's saying that
the daily consistency,

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.521
which seems like a proxy to success,

00:03:18.521 --> 00:03:21.202
isn't the reason it was successful.

00:03:21.202 --> 00:03:23.924
It was more of me and my talents.

00:03:23.924 --> 00:03:28.192
Joe, I think the answer
is they're both correct.

00:03:28.192 --> 00:03:30.406
I mean, I would totally disagree with you

00:03:30.406 --> 00:03:32.356
that, I mean, I lived it.

00:03:32.356 --> 00:03:34.673
If I had quit after the nine-month mark,

00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:36.806
it was me, I did it.

00:03:36.806 --> 00:03:38.756
A lot of things would be different today.

00:03:38.756 --> 00:03:41.275
That just wouldn't have
been a foundational

00:03:41.275 --> 00:03:42.779
piece of my narrative.

00:03:42.779 --> 00:03:45.380
I wouldn't have broken out

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.923
into Web 2.0 culture,

00:03:46.923 --> 00:03:48.346
which would then not have allowed me to

00:03:48.346 --> 00:03:50.557
be a top-25-follows
person during that era.

00:03:50.557 --> 00:03:52.421
Would have not allowed me to network

00:03:52.421 --> 00:03:54.263
in South by Southwest and meet all the

00:03:54.263 --> 00:03:56.418
founders of the future
most important companies.

00:03:56.418 --> 00:03:58.734
There'd surely be no opportunity in 2008

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:00.786
to have dinner with Zucks at South by,

00:04:00.786 --> 00:04:02.371
wouldn't have been asked.

00:04:02.371 --> 00:04:04.768
So, you know, I think that yes,

00:04:04.768 --> 00:04:06.231
and I've said it a bunch of times,

00:04:06.231 --> 00:04:08.100
no marketing and no tactics will

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.619
help you if your product is shit.

00:04:10.619 --> 00:04:12.712
If I wasn't good enough,

00:04:12.712 --> 00:04:15.333
I could still be doing the episodes.

00:04:15.333 --> 00:04:17.243
There's plenty of people that do.

00:04:17.243 --> 00:04:19.029
I mean, you can go watch
on YouTube right now

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:20.452
someone who's been putting out videos

00:04:20.452 --> 00:04:21.855
everyday for the last seven years

00:04:21.855 --> 00:04:24.292
and still has 8,000
subscribers and isn't getting

00:04:24.292 --> 00:04:26.609
any traction cause they're
just not good enough.

00:04:26.609 --> 00:04:29.169
And being good enough is
the variable, number one.

00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:32.399
But to downplay the
consistency of the work ethic.

00:04:32.399 --> 00:04:35.101
And look, I'm feeling it now
with the #AskGaryVee Show.

00:04:35.101 --> 00:04:37.885
You know, in London, seven selfies.

00:04:37.885 --> 00:04:39.754
Right? Seven people, I'm
literally walking the streets,

00:04:39.754 --> 00:04:42.903
"Gary, Gary," I'm feeling
much more brand equity

00:04:42.903 --> 00:04:45.097
because of the content
that I'm putting out.

00:04:45.097 --> 00:04:47.068
And, honestly, I'm feeling it a lot more

00:04:47.068 --> 00:04:48.998
over the last 60 and 90 days than I did

00:04:48.998 --> 00:04:50.522
over the first year of this show.

00:04:50.522 --> 00:04:52.574
Like, momentum is a real thing.

00:04:52.574 --> 00:04:53.630
Even the Jets game.

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:56.881
We were up 27 to 7 and then it

00:04:56.881 --> 00:04:58.506
started getting a little hairy cause

00:04:58.506 --> 00:05:00.335
for the last 18, 20 minutes Miami

00:05:00.335 --> 00:05:01.574
basically had the momentum.

00:05:01.574 --> 00:05:02.550
We held on.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:03.626
Momentum is real.

00:05:03.626 --> 00:05:06.471
In sports and in life and in business.

00:05:06.471 --> 00:05:08.543
And the only way you gain momentum

00:05:08.543 --> 00:05:10.311
is by putting down the foundation of work

00:05:10.311 --> 00:05:12.280
that gives you the chance for momentum.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.882
So momentum just doesn't
come out of thin air.

00:05:14.882 --> 00:05:16.873
It's a play, it's a
moment, it's consistency.

00:05:16.873 --> 00:05:18.376
It's putting in the work and so

00:05:18.376 --> 00:05:21.607
Joe, I don't pound that home because

00:05:21.607 --> 00:05:23.618
I think both matter quite a bit.

00:05:23.618 --> 00:05:25.914
But yes, you know, no marketing solves

00:05:25.914 --> 00:05:28.311
a bad product.

00:05:28.311 --> 00:05:30.912
- [India] From Samantha.
- [Gary] Samantha.

00:05:30.912 --> 00:05:32.822
- How do you feel--
- [Gary] Samantha Fox?

00:05:32.822 --> 00:05:34.488
- Samantha Elizabeth.
- [Gary] Do you even know who

00:05:34.488 --> 00:05:36.520
Samantha Fox is?
- [India] I don't think so?

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.063
- Britt?
- [Britt] I don't think so.

00:05:38.063 --> 00:05:39.688
- DRock?

00:05:39.688 --> 00:05:41.212
- Staphon?

00:05:41.212 --> 00:05:43.284
Go ahead.
- [India] Who is it?

00:05:43.284 --> 00:05:44.747
- Listen.

00:05:44.747 --> 00:05:45.803
Go ahead.

00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:47.855
- [Voiceover] Samantha asks,

00:05:47.855 --> 00:05:49.887
"How do you feel about a
short video in place of

00:05:49.887 --> 00:05:51.858
"your profile picture on Facebook?

00:05:51.858 --> 00:05:54.886
"Motion sick or sick move, you decide."

00:05:54.886 --> 00:05:57.039
- Motion sickness or sick move?

00:05:57.039 --> 00:06:00.270
You know, I think gif culture
is real, people like it.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:03.337
You know, I think
storytelling is storytelling.

00:06:03.337 --> 00:06:05.003
I think they both can work.

00:06:05.003 --> 00:06:07.014
I think it comes down
to how good the gif is.

00:06:07.014 --> 00:06:08.843
You know, like, I think it comes,

00:06:08.843 --> 00:06:11.326
just like how it comes
down to the picture.

00:06:12.366 --> 00:06:15.251
To me, there's more
upside in the fact that

00:06:15.251 --> 00:06:17.933
it just creates a little
bit more creative freedom.

00:06:17.933 --> 00:06:19.741
DRock, is that what you
were emailing me about?

00:06:19.741 --> 00:06:21.183
You want one of those, right?

00:06:21.183 --> 00:06:23.052
Um, yeah, I mean,

00:06:23.052 --> 00:06:26.037
DRock wants it cause
he's all about the video.

00:06:29.907 --> 00:06:32.126
- [India] A lot of people were asking you,

00:06:32.126 --> 00:06:33.426
that's why--
- [Gary] No, I'm glad.

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:36.027
I mean, I appreciate everybody
asking and, you know,

00:06:36.027 --> 00:06:37.856
this is not something I'm
massively passionate about,

00:06:37.856 --> 00:06:39.868
meaning I think this
falls in the same category

00:06:39.868 --> 00:06:41.634
about a company's name.

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:44.744
If your animated gif, your
little video is good and clever,

00:06:44.744 --> 00:06:46.572
it'll capture people's attention,

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:48.745
I think for the short term,
over the next three months

00:06:48.745 --> 00:06:50.026
it's probably a smart tactic.

00:06:50.026 --> 00:06:51.285
Because in the world of the feed

00:06:51.285 --> 00:06:53.033
you'll stand out cause it's new.

00:06:53.033 --> 00:06:54.515
And so I think if you wanna go there

00:06:54.515 --> 00:06:57.299
for a little bit more
awareness, you do it right now.

00:06:57.299 --> 00:06:59.575
As a matter of fact, I'm
gonna now tell DRock to pick

00:06:59.575 --> 00:07:03.394
some part of this answer
to be my motion video

00:07:03.394 --> 00:07:04.878
just for kicks and giggles.

00:07:04.878 --> 00:07:06.340
It'll be very meta.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.043
And so yeah, I think
over the next 3-6 months

00:07:09.043 --> 00:07:10.364
it's a smart tactic that might give you

00:07:10.364 --> 00:07:12.679
a little more uptick in awareness

00:07:12.679 --> 00:07:16.214
in a feed, crowd-noise feed culture.

00:07:16.214 --> 00:07:18.673
But I think it comes down to the video

00:07:18.673 --> 00:07:20.766
or it comes down to the picture.

00:07:20.766 --> 00:07:22.025
One of the biggest reasons,

00:07:22.025 --> 00:07:23.224
one thing I will say is,

00:07:23.224 --> 00:07:25.357
I didn't change my picture on Twitter

00:07:25.357 --> 00:07:27.368
for a very long time,

00:07:27.368 --> 00:07:29.604
even though I no longer
looked like that dude.

00:07:29.604 --> 00:07:31.473
Which I think is a pet peeve of mine.

00:07:31.473 --> 00:07:34.357
But I just couldn't
give up the consistency

00:07:34.357 --> 00:07:36.572
of, you know, you're so visual.

00:07:36.572 --> 00:07:38.116
The amount of times I'm
on Twitter where I'm like,

00:07:38.116 --> 00:07:39.335
"Wait a minute, that's Rick?

00:07:39.335 --> 00:07:40.412
"He must've changed his profile."

00:07:40.412 --> 00:07:42.789
So I would say that I like the idea of

00:07:42.789 --> 00:07:46.162
keeping something consistent
in these small icon worlds

00:07:46.162 --> 00:07:47.929
after you pick something
for a long period of time.

00:07:47.929 --> 00:07:49.046
If you're trying to build a brand,

00:07:49.046 --> 00:07:50.834
if you're trying to get your content seen.

00:07:50.834 --> 00:07:52.886
- [Voiceover] Irina asks,

00:07:52.886 --> 00:07:54.451
"You seem to be everywhere!

00:07:54.451 --> 00:07:56.360
"Are you democratic about
your talks or do you

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:59.591
"have a strategy in place
for public speaking?"

00:07:59.591 --> 00:08:01.766
- Irina, I have a
strategy about everything.

00:08:01.766 --> 00:08:04.468
So I have a strategy
for my public speaking.

00:08:04.468 --> 00:08:06.926
At this point in my career
I try not to speak much

00:08:06.926 --> 00:08:09.709
unless I'm getting paid
my very expensive fee.

00:08:09.709 --> 00:08:12.331
It's a promise I made myself and my wife.

00:08:12.331 --> 00:08:13.528
So there's that.

00:08:13.528 --> 00:08:15.175
But when I am not getting paid,

00:08:15.175 --> 00:08:17.734
it is absolutely a
disproportionate opportunity

00:08:17.734 --> 00:08:19.015
to do one of two things.

00:08:19.015 --> 00:08:21.391
Which is build my brand.

00:08:21.391 --> 00:08:23.810
And if I have to speak where,

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:26.592
I spoke recently where
Hilary Clinton was first

00:08:26.592 --> 00:08:28.563
and Steve Forbes was behind
me and I was in the middle.

00:08:28.563 --> 00:08:29.924
That's good brand positioning.

00:08:29.924 --> 00:08:31.774
Ninety percent of the
crowd, 98% of the crowd,

00:08:31.774 --> 00:08:33.582
they didn't know who I
was, but they sure knew

00:08:33.582 --> 00:08:35.106
that I was one of those three people

00:08:35.106 --> 00:08:36.649
and that means something
and that makes them

00:08:36.649 --> 00:08:38.335
Google me up a little bit more.

00:08:38.335 --> 00:08:40.265
The other thing that I do is that

00:08:40.265 --> 00:08:42.399
I really want to support
certain communities.

00:08:42.399 --> 00:08:45.304
New Jersey tech scene,
the Harlem tech scene

00:08:45.304 --> 00:08:47.905
is something that I'm going
to be doing in the future.

00:08:47.905 --> 00:08:51.379
You know, interns, young people.

00:08:51.379 --> 00:08:53.980
So if there's something that
emotionally feels right to me,

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:55.992
that I know that if I do it,

00:08:55.992 --> 00:08:57.718
people in the future will do it as well

00:08:57.718 --> 00:08:59.973
cause that organization
can leverage my name.

00:08:59.973 --> 00:09:02.289
And I'm giving back to something
that means something to me,

00:09:02.289 --> 00:09:04.179
Jersey, kind of like

00:09:04.179 --> 00:09:07.917
the come-up, underdog
area, you know, youngsters.

00:09:07.917 --> 00:09:12.183
Those are things that
I'll invest my time into.

00:09:12.183 --> 00:09:14.256
But there's no, like, sure!

00:09:14.256 --> 00:09:17.060
No, it's all like, (clicking noise)

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:18.875
No, "Sure!"

00:09:20.879 --> 00:09:22.586
- [Voiceover] Matthew asks,

00:09:22.586 --> 00:09:24.882
"What's the next industry
to be turned upside down

00:09:24.882 --> 00:09:26.832
"because they aren't
adjusting business models

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:29.311
"to fit changes in technology?"

00:09:29.311 --> 00:09:31.525
- I mean, the answer is everything.

00:09:31.525 --> 00:09:33.680
You need the breakout product.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.670
The reason the hotel industry

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.027
and the limousine service
industry have been

00:09:38.027 --> 00:09:39.510
thrown upside down is because

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.151
entrepreneurs came and
attacked it full-throttle.

00:09:42.151 --> 00:09:44.875
You know, the refrigerator, appliances,

00:09:44.875 --> 00:09:48.388
smart appliances are coming
so all appliances are on-call.

00:09:48.388 --> 00:09:50.054
Retail.

00:09:50.054 --> 00:09:52.635
Brands now can go direct to consumer.

00:09:52.635 --> 00:09:54.647
So, you know, I think
we'll see that play out

00:09:54.647 --> 00:09:56.149
over the next 10 years.

00:09:56.149 --> 00:09:58.385
TV, big media conglomerate companies

00:09:58.385 --> 00:10:01.473
have to adjust to the
over-the-top networks

00:10:01.473 --> 00:10:03.870
and all the infrastructure
they have in place

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:08.076
to be very expensive to produce
television-like products

00:10:08.076 --> 00:10:10.514
when people can just do it like this.

00:10:10.514 --> 00:10:14.172
Or over-the-top as a
new distribution game.

00:10:14.172 --> 00:10:16.853
The internet is the middle man.

00:10:16.853 --> 00:10:17.767
Period.

00:10:17.767 --> 00:10:19.372
And so anybody who was in the middle

00:10:19.372 --> 00:10:20.896
is on-call.

00:10:20.896 --> 00:10:22.461
And that's most things.

00:10:22.461 --> 00:10:26.301
And so I think every industry
is prime for disruption.

00:10:26.301 --> 00:10:29.287
It's why I'm so excited
about this generation.

